---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import LinkAnatomyAnalyzer from '../../components/LinkAnatomyAnalyzer';
---

<Layout title="Link Anatomy Analyzer" description="Learn how to read a URL and spot advanced phishing links before you click.">
  <div class="bg-indigo-50 py-12 border-b border-indigo-100">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 text-center">
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-indigo-900 tracking-tight mb-4">
        Anatomy of a Link
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-indigo-800">
        Paste a suspicious URL below. We will break it apart and show you exactly where it is trying to send you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    <LinkAnatomyAnalyzer client:load />

    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-12 grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">The Subdomain Trick</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">
          When you buy a domain name (like <code>example.com</code>), you get to create as many "subdomains" as you want for free. They go on the <em>left</em> side of the root domain.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-600">
          So, if a hacker buys <code>secure-update.net</code>, they can create a subdomain called <code>paypal.com</code>. The resulting link looks like: <br/><br/>
          <strong class="bg-red-50 text-red-700 px-2 py-1 rounded border border-red-200">paypal.com.secure-update.net</strong><br/><br/>
          Your eyes naturally read left-to-right, so you see "paypal" and trust it. But the internet reads right-to-left. <strong>The word just before the .com or .net is the only one that matters.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">The Homoglyph Trick</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">
          Sometimes hackers don't even bother with subdomains. They just register a domain name that looks identical to the real one.
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-600 font-mono text-sm mb-4">
          <li>microsoft.com <span class="text-slate-400 font-sans text-xs ml-2">(Real)</span></li>
          <li class="text-red-600">rnicrosoft.com <span class="text-slate-400 font-sans text-xs ml-2">(Fake: r + n)</span></li>
        </ul>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-600 font-mono text-sm">
          <li>apple.com <span class="text-slate-400 font-sans text-xs ml-2">(Real)</span></li>
          <li class="text-red-600">appIe.com <span class="text-slate-400 font-sans text-xs ml-2">(Fake: Capital I)</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
